<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lugalbanda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lugalbanda was a legendary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, said to be the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> and the husband of the goddess Ninsun. Two Sumerian epic poems tell of his adventures in the mountains while campaigning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratta">Aratta</a>, including his encounter with the Anzu bird. He was later worshipped as a god at Uruk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugalbanda.jpg" alt="Lugalbanda" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Obverse._The_story_of_Lugalbanda_in_the_Mountain_Cave,_Old-Babylonian_period,_from_southern_Iraq._Sulaymaniyah_Museum,_Iraqi_Kurdistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Lugalbanda was a deified Sumerian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> who, according to various sources of Mesopotamian literature, was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> names him as the second king of Uruk, gives him a reign of 1,200 years and the epithet &quot;the Shepherd&quot;. His historicity is uncertain among scholars, and attempts to date him to the ED II period rest on an amalgam of second-millennium epic traditions and unclear archaeological observations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>He appears in Sumerian literature as early as the mid-3rd millennium, in the incomplete text Lugalbanda and Ninsuna from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh">Abu Salabikh</a>, which describes a romance with Ninsun. In the earliest god-lists from Fara he ranks much lower than the goddess, but later traditions down to the Seleucid period often list the two together. His worship as a god appears from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> period, in tablets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puzrish-dagan">Puzrish-Dagan</a>, and in the Old Babylonian period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-kashid">Sin-kashid</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> built a temple for Lugalbanda and Ninsun and made his daughter Niši-īnī-šu their priestess. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> called Lugalbanda and Ninsun their holy parents and themselves brothers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>.</p><p>Two Sumerian stories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> period, Lugalbanda in the Mountain Cave and Lugalbanda and the Anzu Bird, make him the hero. They belong to a series on the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and Ensuhkeshdanna, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratta">Aratta</a>, presumably in the Iranian highlands, and show Lugalbanda as a soldier in Enmerkar's army. Both are known only in later versions of the 18th century BCE, and the fragments do not say he succeeded Enmerkar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a> and the earlier Sumerian stories, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> calls himself the son of Lugalbanda and Ninsun. In Gilgamesh and Huwawa he swears by the life of his mother Ninsun and his father, holy Lugalbanda. In Akkadian versions he calls Lugalbanda his personal god and presents him the oil-filled horns of the defeated Bull of Heaven. Scholars including Bendt Alster and Jennifer Larson have studied the early epic tradition around him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lugalbanda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Early Dynastic period Uruk’s rulers used the title lugal, king, and the Sumerian King List names a First Dynasty of Uruk including Meskiaggasher, Enmerkar, <b>Lugalbanda</b> and Gilgamesh, who may have lived around 2700 BCE, though none of these early kings is attested in contemporary inscriptions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…was copied in the schools of the early 2nd millennium BCE: epics of the heroes of Uruk, Enmerkar, <b>Lugalbanda</b> and Bilgamesh, better known as Gilgamesh, whose adventures in five Sumerian poems were later drawn on for the Akkadian Epic of Gilgamesh; myths such as the Descent of Inanna…</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
